Another idea.  These are the "dungeons of doom", right?  So where are
the dungeons?  Someone requested more special levels - how about a
"prison" style level, or even just a special room.  You might be locked
away via a teleport trap, trap door, etc.  Doors should be unkickable.
If done as an early level, let the player keep his stuff.  Keys, lock
picks, knock spells, etc., would get you out.

If done later in the game, have the teleport-like trap also take all
belongings and store them in the next room.  To escape, either use
known knock spells, or wait for the "keeper" (Orcs?) to come feed you
(next time you get hungry) and defeat it (bare hands and spells) to
escape.  There would be more orcs in the next room with your backpack.
They probably even went through it already!

------------------------------------------------------------------

YAI:  Keys, lockpicks and credit cards have never made much sense to
me.  They all always work!  Credit cards should be the worst, should
take the most time to use, and should fail frequently (but allow
retries).  They should not work at all with some locks (see below).

Keys should come in flavors.  There should be, say, eight or so
different keys (kept internally, but not known to the player unless
identified), and the same number of matching locks.  A player would
have to collect a set of keys, differentiating between them by trial
and error or by ID spells.  A new item might be a keychain, allowing
it plus multiple keys to take one pack slot.

	a)pply what? D
	Which key would you like to use? [a-d or ESC] a
	In what direction? k
	Unlock it? [y/n] y
	The key won't fit!  Try another? [a-d or ESC]

Each try should only use one turn.  Oh, and get rid of the unnecessary
"unlock it?" line!

Lock picks should take an intermediate amount of time, and have a
semi-random success function based on lock complexity, Int and Dex.
This should be retryable.  They should also be less common.

I mentioned lock complexity.  Since we will have different lock types,
we might as well add a complexity level to each type.  Have more
complex locks (and their keys) be more likely lower in the dungeon.
